<Hub pillarId="ai-basics">
  <p>A pitch that cannot show its document does not get repeated here. The definition is short. The machine is not a person, and a smooth paragraph is not a receipt.</p>
  <h2>What is an AI system?</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles">OECD AI Principles</a>, updated in May 2024, define an AI system as a machine-based system that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers from the input it receives how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations, or decisions. Those outputs can influence physical or virtual environments. Systems differ in how independently they act, and in how much they adapt after they are deployed.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the whole idea in one sentence. A spell-checker, a photo app that names a face, and a chatbot are all systems in that family. They are not the same product, and they are not a person.
  </p>
  <h2>What is a model?</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="https://internationalaisafetyreport.org/sites/default/files/2026-02/international-ai-safety-report-2026.pdf">International AI Safety Report 2026</a> calls a model a computer program that processes inputs to do tasks such as prediction, classification, or generation. Most models today learn from data rather than being written as a long list of hand-made rules. A system is the model plus the pieces around it, such as a chat window.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same report’s glossary separates narrow AI, built for one task such as chess, from general-purpose AI, which can do a variety of tasks. The chatbot people meet first is usually the general-purpose kind.
  </p>
  <h2>Why can a fluent answer be wrong?</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.AI.600-1">NIST’s Generative AI Profile</a> uses the word confabulation for confidently stated content that is erroneous or false. People also say “hallucination.” NIST notes that large language models predict the next token or word. That statistical step can be factually right, and it can be factually wrong. The risk shows up when a reader believes the confident sentence.
  </p>
  <p>The finished guide is <a href="/articles/what-is-ai/">What is AI, in plain English?</a> Words live in the <a href="/glossary/">glossary</a>.</p>
